[{:db/id 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/math-academy-id "q-109777" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$, that is, the rows of ${A}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${A}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${A}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65c8f326-9093-58bd-a6e1-bf36f4cf4e21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65c8f326-9093-58bd-a6e1-bf36f4cf4e21" :answer-field/id #uuid "65c8f326-9093-58bd-a6e1-bf36f4cf4e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bc41a449-214b-5c1b-8a85-1c8dc86ad746" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f62c90f5-3a7e-558e-82d7-d3e2ac3e06e4" "answer-f4e3cd61-e48f-5faf-ad9f-c294bdd8ce47" "answer-61b8af3e-3b47-5aa2-91bd-6b1c4c03c696" "answer-bc41a449-214b-5c1b-8a85-1c8dc86ad746" "answer-a0b64355-3c4a-5468-b79c-60409857f476"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f62c90f5-3a7e-558e-82d7-d3e